was the Dataflow team--

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:54.587
so Google Cloud Dataflow
team reached out to us.

00:08:54.587 --> 00:08:55.420
And they said, look.

00:08:55.420 --> 00:08:56.906
We've got this new
product in beta.

00:08:56.906 --> 00:08:58.030
It's called Cloud Dataflow.

00:08:58.030 --> 00:09:00.080
We think it would be
pretty cool for you guys

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:02.390
to be beta testers on it.

00:09:02.390 --> 00:09:04.092
So we said, yeah, absolutely.

00:09:04.092 --> 00:09:04.800
And we jumped in.

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:05.925
We started playing with it.

00:09:05.925 --> 00:09:06.950
We gave them feedback.

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:10.230
And then after that,
the product manager

00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:16.720
for Google Cloud Dataflow, a
guy called Rafael Fernandez,

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:20.330
he then nominated us to become
Google Developer experts.

00:09:20.330 --> 00:09:22.590
And that's the key to being
a Google Developer expert.

00:09:22.590 --> 00:09:25.630
You have to be nominated
by a Google employee.

00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:27.740
So once you're nominated
by a Google employee,

00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:29.350
then you do an application.

00:09:29.350 --> 00:09:30.990
So myself and Pablo did that.

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:33.530
And then you go through
a round of interviews.

00:09:33.530 --> 00:09:35.530
They make sure you really
know what you're doing

00:09:35.530 --> 00:09:37.450
and you really know what
you're talking about.

00:09:37.450 --> 00:09:40.440
And then you finally get
accepted to the program.

00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:41.920
And that's pretty
much the journey

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:43.669
of how we became Google
Developer experts.

00:09:43.669 --> 00:09:44.720
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:47.009
MARK MANDEL: So Pablo,
you've been relatively quiet.

00:09:47.009 --> 00:09:49.050
Why don't you tell us a
little bit about the work

00:09:49.050 --> 00:09:53.040
you do at Shine, the tools you
use, and how they go together,

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:54.590
and what you're up to?

00:09:54.590 --> 00:09:55.265
PABLO CAIF: Yes.

00:09:55.265 --> 00:09:58.040
So first of all, Shine
is a software consultancy

00:09:58.040 --> 00:10:00.980
based in Melbourne.

00:10:00.980 --> 00:10:04.650
And we do a lot of
work with Big Data.

00:10:07.430 --> 00:10:09.751
Our main client is Telstra.

00:10:09.751 --> 00:10:10.250
Yeah.

00:10:10.250 --> 00:10:12.220
It's one of the largest ISP.

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:14.642
And we'll talk a
little bit later

00:10:14.642 --> 00:10:17.100
about more in-depth about the
stuff we've been doing there.

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:24.840
But basically, we need to enter
in just tons and tons of Atom

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:27.380
logs and process them.

00:10:27.380 --> 00:10:31.890
We use a whole combination
of stuff between Google Cloud

00:10:31.890 --> 00:10:35.240
Platform tools and a
bit of AWS as well.

00:10:35.240 --> 00:10:40.769
Basically, the idea is to
help them to understand

00:10:40.769 --> 00:10:42.560
all the data they're
using to basically get

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:46.380
like useful insights to all
the information they have.

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:47.630
MARK MANDEL: That's very cool.

00:10:47.630 --> 00:10:48.338
That's very cool.

00:10:48.338 --> 00:10:50.360
So I think one of
the highlights for me

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:52.806
when actually I
probably got to know you

00:10:52.806 --> 00:10:55.660
and Graham from Shine
Technology and stuff,

00:10:55.660 --> 00:10:58.850
was the blog post about the
17-minute train challenge.

00:10:58.850 --> 00:10:59.920
GRAHAM POLLEY: Oh, yes.

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:01.710
MARK MANDEL: Which I
thought was quite exciting.

00:11:01.710 --> 00:11:03.150
Do you want to
tell our listeners

00:11:03.150 --> 00:11:05.400
a little bit about that,
and how that came to be.

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:08.035
And I mean, even like where
did that idea come from?

00:11:08.035 --> 00:11:09.118
GRAHAM POLLEY: Yeah, sure.

00:11:11.770 --> 00:11:15.240
So I take the train to work.

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:17.490
And I was sitting on
the train coming in.

00:11:17.490 --> 00:11:21.620
I was reading, as you
do, the tech news.

00:11:21.620 --> 00:11:23.090
And something popped
up in my feed.

00:11:23.090 --> 00:11:28.820
And it was Google have announced
a new product called Dataproc.

00:11:28.820 --> 00:11:31.060
I went oh, that
looks kind of cool.

00:11:31.060 --> 00:11:33.250
So I started reading about it.

00:11:33.250 --> 00:11:38.437
And I was actually chatting with
Pablo at the same time on IM.

00:11:38.437 --> 00:11:39.770
We just got to talking about it.

00:11:39.770 --> 00:11:42.159
And then one of the things
in the announcemen--

00:11:42.159 --> 00:11:43.950
I think it was on the
Google Cloud Platform

00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:48.280
blog-- it was, we can
spin up a Hadoop or Spark

00:11:48.280 --> 00:11:49.936
cluster in under 90 seconds.

00:11:49.936 --> 00:11:51.320
And I thought, wow.

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:52.570
That's a bold claim.

00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:54.840
That's a really bold claim.

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:55.900
And we got to thinking.

00:11:55.900 --> 00:11:58.240
And we thought, let's vet that.

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:00.350
Let's see if it
really can do that.

00:12:00.350 --> 00:12:05.040
So we came up with the idea
of could we actually spin up

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:11.180
a Hadoop/Spark cluster using
Dataproc in the time it takes

00:12:11.180 --> 00:12:13.840
for my train to
depart Melbourne City

00:12:13.840 --> 00:12:16.120
and get home to my last stop.

00:12:16.120 --> 00:12:19.650
But not only that, so we thought
that was a little bit easy.

00:12:19.650 --> 00:12:23.630
We also threw a few
curve balls into the mix.

00:12:23.630 --> 00:12:26.870
So it would have to
spin up the cluster.

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:29.040
My train commute is 17 minutes.

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:31.100
So it would have to
spin up the cluster.

00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:33.770
It would have to be able
to run a Hadoop job.

00:12:33.770 --> 00:12:36.820
It would have to be able
to run Apache Spark job.

00:12:36.820 --> 00:12:40.870
And I should also be
able to SSH into some

00:12:40.870 --> 00:12:43.770
of the nodes in the cluster
and have a play with them,

00:12:43.770 --> 00:12:47.245
and use the Cloud
Shell from the browser,

00:12:47.245 --> 00:12:48.370
and all that kind of stuff.

00:12:48.370 --> 00:12:51.520
And then finally, tear
down the whole cluster

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:54.670
before the train got
into the last station.

00:12:54.670 --> 00:12:57.450
So that's really how
the idea came about.

00:12:57.450 --> 00:12:59.849
It was just something Pablo
were chatting about it.

00:12:59.849 --> 00:13:02.140
We thought it would be really
cool, something original.

00:13:02.140 --> 00:13:03.750
And it would actually
be a good way

00:13:03.750 --> 00:13:06.250
to see if Dataproc
really was that fast.

00:13:06.250 --> 00:13:09.650
Could it really stand up
a cluster that quickly?

00:13:09.650 --> 00:13:12.540
And so we did it.

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:14.680
One of the great things
about it is actually we

00:13:14.680 --> 00:13:16.290
just tethered it off my phone.

00:13:16.290 --> 00:13:17.206
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:13:17.206 --> 00:13:19.550
GRAHAM POLLEY: So we stood
up a Dataproc cluster.

00:13:19.550 --> 00:13:22.570
I can't remember, I
think it was 8 nodes

00:13:22.570 --> 00:13:26.370
in the cluster, or something
like that all tethering off 4G.

00:13:26.370 --> 00:13:28.491
It's crazy.

00:13:28.491 --> 00:13:30.490
MARK MANDEL: I do see
here that you didn't quite

00:13:30.490 --> 00:13:32.010
complete the challenge.

00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:34.190
You got very close, though.

00:13:34.190 --> 00:13:35.830
GRAHAM POLLEY: Got
very, very close.

00:13:35.830 --> 00:13:39.170
So I was pulling into
the last station.

00:13:39.170 --> 00:13:43.340
And I had initiated a
shutdown of the cluster.

00:13:43.340 --> 00:13:46.580
And unfortunately, the cluster
just didn't shut down in time.

00:13:46.580 --> 00:13:47.930
So I got to spin it up.

00:13:47.930 --> 00:13:50.240
I got to run a Hadoop job.

00:13:50.240 --> 00:13:52.210
I got to run a spark job.

00:13:52.210 --> 00:13:55.290
SSH into some of the nodes.

00:13:55.290 --> 00:13:57.070
But the cluster just
didn't come down

00:13:57.070 --> 00:13:59.500
in the time it needed
before I got home.

00:13:59.500 --> 00:14:02.448
But I marked it
down as a success.

00:14:02.448 --> 00:14:04.964
MARK MANDEL: So it was deleting.

00:14:04.964 --> 00:14:05.880
It was in the process.

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:08.570
GRAHAM POLLEY: It was in the
process of deleting, exactly.

00:14:08.570 --> 00:14:11.567
I think I missed it
by about 30 seconds.

00:14:11.567 --> 00:14:12.650
MARK MANDEL: There you go.

00:14:12.650 --> 00:14:13.691
GRAHAM POLLEY: Seriously.

00:14:13.691 --> 00:14:14.700
So yeah.

00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:16.090
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Was it recorded?

00:14:16.090 --> 00:14:18.790
I'd have loved to see that as
a video of seeing you panicking

00:14:18.790 --> 00:14:21.230
and trying to stop the cluster
right before the train's

00:14:21.230 --> 00:14:22.560
arriving into the station.

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:24.530
GRAHAM POLLEY: Yeah.

00:14:24.530 --> 00:14:27.260
So unfortunately,
it wasn't recorded.

00:14:27.260 --> 00:14:29.230
Didn't have the
foresight for that one.

00:14:29.230 --> 00:14:30.760
Did do a blog post about it.

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:33.070
So you're more than
welcome to read the blog.

00:14:33.070 --> 00:14:35.100
And it's got
screenshots in there.

00:14:35.100 --> 00:14:36.974
And it was a very uber
scientific challenge,

00:14:36.974 --> 00:14:37.640
as you can tell.

00:14:37.640 --> 00:14:39.650
Jump on the train,
tether a phone,

00:14:39.650 --> 00:14:40.960
spin up a Dataproc cluster.

00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:43.496
But one of the things
that I failed to consider,

00:14:43.496 --> 00:14:45.120
or that we failed to
consider before we

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:51.145
did it was that there's black
spots on the train with 4G.

00:14:51.145 --> 00:14:53.230
So every now and then,
I'd lose my connection

00:14:53.230 --> 00:14:54.840
and I'd lose my SSH session.

00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:58.670
And like, panic, panic,
panic, until my connectivity

00:14:58.670 --> 00:14:59.340
came back.

00:14:59.340 --> 00:15:00.580
And yeah.

00:15:00.580 --> 00:15:01.580
But it was good fun.

00:15:01.580 --> 00:15:02.560
It was good fun.

00:15:02.560 --> 00:15:05.670
And again, it was the
originality of it as well.

00:15:05.670 --> 00:15:08.050
And the blog post got
quite a lot of attention.

00:15:08.050 --> 00:15:10.260
And I think it got shared
again internally at Google.

00:15:10.260 --> 00:15:11.400
So that's really cool.

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:14.526
And that leads into the
Google Developer expert stuff.

00:15:14.526 --> 00:15:16.150
That's the kind of
stuff we do as well.

00:15:16.150 --> 00:15:17.630
Writing blog posts like that.

00:15:17.630 --> 00:15:19.130
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
And you were saying

00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:22.360
that it's not a very
scientific method of comparing

00:15:22.360 --> 00:15:24.690
performance.

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:26.800
I have this video
that I really like

00:15:26.800 --> 00:15:31.500
of one of the people
from [INAUDIBLE] team,

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:36.050
Brendan Burns, trying to measure
how long it takes to start up

00:15:36.050 --> 00:15:38.130
a [INAUDIBLE] cluster,
and comparing it

00:15:38.130 --> 00:15:40.470
to how long it takes for
him to prepare a latte.

00:15:40.470 --> 00:15:41.220
GRAHAM POLLEY: Oh.

00:15:41.220 --> 00:15:42.240
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So you know--

00:15:42.240 --> 00:15:43.750
PABLO CAIF: Sounds
like a lot of fun.

00:15:43.750 --> 00:15:44.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:44.983
MARK MANDEL: It's
entirely scientific.

00:15:44.983 --> 00:15:45.910
Entirely.

00:15:45.910 --> 00:15:48.812
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Entirely
scientific, of course.

00:15:48.812 --> 00:15:49.770
MARK MANDEL: All right.

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:50.560
Cool.

00:15:50.560 --> 00:15:53.420
So obviously, you're doing
stuff with like Dataproc.

00:15:53.420 --> 00:15:56.536
And you're doing stuff
with Big Data and BigQuery.

00:15:56.536 --> 00:15:58.910
Do you want to talk a bit more
about the sort of Big Data

00:15:58.910 --> 00:16:00.000
problems you're working on.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:01.360
Obviously, you said you
were talking about analytics

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:02.310
and stuff like that.

00:16:02.310 --> 00:16:04.640
But can you talk a bit more
concretely about the stuff

00:16:04.640 --> 00:16:07.420
that you're building and
why you chose Google Cloud

00:16:07.420 --> 00:16:11.230
Platform and the tools you
have to solve those problems.

00:16:11.230 --> 00:16:14.360
PABLO CAIF: So I mentioned
before, we're basically

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:17.360
dealing with tons and
tons of [INAUDIBLE] logs.

00:16:17.360 --> 00:16:23.450
What we have come to work on is
basically a statistical model

00:16:23.450 --> 00:16:26.750
that analyzes all those
logs, which is developed

00:16:26.750 --> 00:16:30.310
by a veteran, not a scientist.

00:16:30.310 --> 00:16:33.120
And that's the main
beat we actually

00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:38.750
started using Dataflow for--
all the sort of ETL work

00:16:38.750 --> 00:16:42.010
and the actual statistical
model implementation,

00:16:42.010 --> 00:16:43.480
each one of the steps.

00:16:43.480 --> 00:16:47.030
And we found that it was
very helpful in that aspect.

00:16:47.030 --> 00:16:50.460
And we also use
BigQuery, of course,

00:16:50.460 --> 00:16:54.156
to store all these
huge mound of data.

00:16:54.156 --> 00:16:57.390
And we can run queries
pretty quickly,

00:16:57.390 --> 00:16:59.670
like go through
terabytes of data

00:16:59.670 --> 00:17:05.170
in a matter of seconds, which
I remember at the beginning,

00:17:05.170 --> 00:17:08.800
left a few people
very, very astonished

00:17:08.800 --> 00:17:10.099
about all this stuff.

00:17:10.099 --> 00:17:13.990
Like wow, I can't
believe-- you're

00:17:13.990 --> 00:17:19.170
telling me you can actually
run query terabytes of data

00:17:19.170 --> 00:17:20.400
in 15 seconds?

00:17:20.400 --> 00:17:21.230
Yeah.

00:17:21.230 --> 00:17:26.329
But with Oracle, it takes
like-- I don't know-- one hour,

00:17:26.329 --> 00:17:27.150
you know.

00:17:27.150 --> 00:17:28.030
And then when they
were seeing it,

00:17:28.030 --> 00:17:29.765
they were like, oh,
I can't believe this.

00:17:29.765 --> 00:17:30.931
MARK MANDEL: So I'm curious.

00:17:30.931 --> 00:17:34.490
What's the largest data set that
you've put through big query?

00:17:34.490 --> 00:17:35.790
GRAHAM POLLEY: So yeah.

00:17:35.790 --> 00:17:39.540
So I think the amount of
data that we have [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:39.540 --> 00:17:41.750
is what do we have?

00:17:41.750 --> 00:17:45.930
So we're doing about 5
billion rows a month.

00:17:45.930 --> 00:17:48.010
That's what we were
ingesting into BigQuery.

00:17:48.010 --> 00:17:49.110
Is that right, Pablo?

00:17:49.110 --> 00:17:50.620
About 5 billion rows a month.

00:17:50.620 --> 00:17:52.990
And then we've run queries
over several years.

00:17:52.990 --> 00:17:56.940
So we've gone up to
about 100 billion rows.

00:17:56.940 --> 00:17:59.230
And it was about 15 terabytes.

00:17:59.230 --> 00:18:00.000
PABLO CAIF: Yeah.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:02.570
It's difficult, because we've
got several projects going on.

00:18:02.570 --> 00:18:06.230
So you sort of need
to get on all of them.

00:18:06.230 --> 00:18:08.500
But I think something like that.

00:18:08.500 --> 00:18:10.640
So for us, it's
quite a lot of data.

00:18:10.640 --> 00:18:11.390
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:18:11.390 --> 00:18:12.630
PABLO CAIF: A lot of data.

00:18:12.630 --> 00:18:13.380
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:18:13.380 --> 00:18:16.250
And so what sort of things are
you providing for the customer?

00:18:16.250 --> 00:18:19.497
Obviously you said, like
statistical models and stuff.

00:18:19.497 --> 00:18:20.830
You're helping them with things?

00:18:23.434 --> 00:18:24.100
PABLO CAIF: Yes.

00:18:24.100 --> 00:18:31.260
So basically we have implemented
all these statistical model.

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:34.200
Then they use like BI
tools to access to that.

00:18:34.200 --> 00:18:36.720
So all the results
of running the model

00:18:36.720 --> 00:18:38.670
get stamped into big query.

00:18:38.670 --> 00:18:43.130
And we also have been
trying to help them lately

00:18:43.130 --> 00:18:48.160
to understand how Dataflow
works, because it's always

00:18:48.160 --> 00:18:54.410
good for them to get a very good
understanding of how they can

00:18:54.410 --> 00:18:56.850
optimize the
algorithms so they can

00:18:56.850 --> 00:19:00.323
work much better in this
Dataflow programming model.

00:19:04.270 --> 00:19:07.970
And we also been giving a few
technical talks about big query

00:19:07.970 --> 00:19:12.240
and data flow to help them
understand the advantages

00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:16.560
you can get when using the
Google Cloud Platform products.

00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:19.060
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It seems like
you've basically been working

00:19:19.060 --> 00:19:21.080
on Big Data for quite a while.

00:19:21.080 --> 00:19:22.950
And you've been
blogging about it.

00:19:22.950 --> 00:19:25.480
And thanks to just
blogging, you ended up

00:19:25.480 --> 00:19:27.580
becoming one of our GDs.

00:19:27.580 --> 00:19:29.850
What is your advice
for other people

00:19:29.850 --> 00:19:31.267
that want to become a GD?

00:19:31.267 --> 00:19:32.100
GRAHAM POLLEY: Yeah.

00:19:32.100 --> 00:19:34.580
That's a really good question.

00:19:34.580 --> 00:19:37.774
So I would say it's
a very good program.

00:19:37.774 --> 00:19:39.440
At least I can speak
for myself and say,

00:19:39.440 --> 00:19:40.530
I'm happy in the program.

00:19:40.530 --> 00:19:43.790
And what I like most about
it, like I touched on earlier,

00:19:43.790 --> 00:19:47.080
would be getting direct access
to the engineering teams.

00:19:47.080 --> 00:19:48.680
That's just awesome.

00:19:48.680 --> 00:19:53.170
Like last month we were
over in San Francisco

00:19:53.170 --> 00:19:57.210
attending the GDE summit, which
is that yearly event that I

00:19:57.210 --> 00:20:01.660
talked about, where we all come
together for a couple of days.

00:20:01.660 --> 00:20:03.950
But around that we
actually went out

00:20:03.950 --> 00:20:07.100
and we met the-- myself
and Pablo went and met all

00:20:07.100 --> 00:20:09.370
the Dataflow engineering teams.

00:20:09.370 --> 00:20:10.370
And we sat with them.

00:20:10.370 --> 00:20:13.330
And we discussed how we used
the tool and the product.

00:20:13.330 --> 00:20:15.180
And that was really cool.

00:20:15.180 --> 00:20:16.430
And they actually sat with us.

00:20:16.430 --> 00:20:18.900
And they actually
did a code review.

00:20:18.900 --> 00:20:21.510
So the tech lead for
data flow reviewed

00:20:21.510 --> 00:20:23.740
our code-- Frances Parry.

00:20:23.740 --> 00:20:26.760
That was just unparalleled
in terms of awesomeness.

00:20:26.760 --> 00:20:29.550
And she was like, you could
do some optimization here,

00:20:29.550 --> 00:20:31.930
performance enhancements
here, and change this,

00:20:31.930 --> 00:20:33.610
and it'd be a little
bit more modular.

00:20:33.610 --> 00:20:36.840
We were like, oh my god, trying
to actually code while she

00:20:36.840 --> 00:20:38.607
was telling us this stuff.

00:20:38.607 --> 00:20:40.190
And then after that,
we went up and we

00:20:40.190 --> 00:20:44.030
met the Dremel and BigQuery
engineering teams in Seattle.

00:20:44.030 --> 00:20:46.210
Like, that's just cool, right?

00:20:46.210 --> 00:20:48.910
Where you can sit with these
guys, and talk to them,

00:20:48.910 --> 00:20:51.560
and talk to engineers,
being engineers

00:20:51.560 --> 00:20:53.560
ourselves, about how
these tools work.

00:20:53.560 --> 00:20:56.305
They don't give up all the
juicy details of course,

00:20:56.305 --> 00:20:59.760
of how, for example, Dremel
works under the hood.

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:02.440
But we were privy
to some information

00:21:02.440 --> 00:21:04.140
that not a lot of
people would get.

00:21:04.140 --> 00:21:04.526
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Gotcha.

00:21:04.526 --> 00:21:05.300
That's very cool.

00:21:05.300 --> 00:21:07.383
GRAHAM POLLEY: And that's
one thing I particularly

00:21:07.383 --> 00:21:09.510
like about the program.

00:21:09.510 --> 00:21:11.700
The second part-- how do
people get into the program

00:21:11.700 --> 00:21:12.840
if they are interested.

00:21:12.840 --> 00:21:16.050
It would really be getting
your name out there.

00:21:16.050 --> 00:21:19.010
So start off doing blogging.

00:21:19.010 --> 00:21:21.970
Go to your local GDG
group in your city.

00:21:21.970 --> 00:21:24.350
So most cities will
have a GDG, which

00:21:24.350 --> 00:21:26.667
is Google Developer Group.

00:21:26.667 --> 00:21:28.250
That's officially
sponsored by Google.

00:21:28.250 --> 00:21:30.690
Going to the meetups.

00:21:30.690 --> 00:21:35.330
Chatting with the guys and
girls at those meetups.

00:21:35.330 --> 00:21:37.950
And then if you're
really, really

00:21:37.950 --> 00:21:42.000
keen to get into the
program, you really

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:45.810
got to make that connection
with someone in Google,

00:21:45.810 --> 00:21:47.020
or be nominated.

00:21:47.020 --> 00:21:48.270
That's the only way to get in.

00:21:48.270 --> 00:21:49.890
And I think that's
a good system,

00:21:49.890 --> 00:21:53.640
because that's the way Google
really get good people in.

00:21:53.640 --> 00:21:55.450
It's if you're nominated.

00:21:55.450 --> 00:21:56.890
So yeah.

00:21:56.890 --> 00:21:59.910
My advice would be
just to get publicity,

00:21:59.910 --> 00:22:03.390
and to get your name out
there blogging, doing talks.

00:22:03.390 --> 00:22:05.050
Because like you
don't have to be a GDE

00:22:05.050 --> 00:22:06.216
to go out and do this stuff.

00:22:06.216 --> 00:22:09.260
You can go out and do this
stuff without being a GDE.

00:22:09.260 --> 00:22:09.870
Right.

00:22:09.870 --> 00:22:12.010
So you can give a talk
at a local meetup.

00:22:12.010 --> 00:22:14.670
Or you can write
a blog on BigQuery

00:22:14.670 --> 00:22:19.050
or [INAUDIBLE] or
Dataflow or Dataproc.

00:22:19.050 --> 00:22:20.460
You don't have to be a GDE.

00:22:20.460 --> 00:22:25.630
And then if it's a good blog
and it's got good content,

00:22:25.630 --> 00:22:28.740
I'm sure will be
picked up by someone.

00:22:28.740 --> 00:22:31.710
At least that's how
it started for us.

00:22:31.710 --> 00:22:34.084
So I hope that
answers your question.

